🟠 Important: Calling `setOrderedChanges([])` here causes a race condition where components briefly render with empty data before the async `result.refetch()` completes.

**Impact**: Users will see a flash of empty state (old data → empty → loading → new data) instead of a smooth transition (old data → loading → new data).

**Suggested fix**: Remove the `setOrderedChanges([])` line. Setting `previousDataRef.current = null` is sufficient to force a rebuild in the useEffect after refetch completes:

```suggestion
  const refetch = React.useCallback(() => {
    // Reset local ordering state to force rebuilding from the latest query result.
    previousDataRef.current = null;
    return result.refetch();
  }, [result.refetch]);
```

This way, old data remains visible during the refetch, and the useEffect will rebuild from scratch when new data arrives (since `previousDataRef.current` is null).

🔴 Critical: `onError` is not supported in TanStack Query v5 (this project uses v5.90.19). This callback was removed in v5 and only exists for mutations, not queries. This code will not execute as intended.

The correct approach is to use a `useEffect` to watch the error property:

```typescript
const user = useQuery({
  queryKey: ["user"],
  queryFn: UserService.getUser,
  enabled: shouldFetchUser,
  retry: false,
  staleTime: 1000 * 60 * 5,
  gcTime: 1000 * 60 * 15,
});

React.useEffect(() => {
  if (user.error && (user.error as any).response?.status === 401) {
    logout.mutate();
  }
}, [user.error, logout]);
```

See: https://tanstack.com/query/latest/docs/framework/react/guides/migrating-to-v5#removed-callbacks-on-usequery

