### Decision: **Valid — applied.**

`Access-Control-Request-Headers` is defined by Fetch as a comma-separated list of header field names ([Fetch spec §4.10](https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/#cors-preflight-fetch)). A client sending a legitimate custom header that happens to *contain* the substring `authorization` (e.g. `x-my-authorization-token`) would have been mis-routed to the permissive CORS path. That would not have been an exploitable hole (the request still has to carry an API key for the real call to succeed), but it would have given browsers wildcard CORS for what should have been a strict-origin request — confusing and worth fixing.

### Changes

**`enterprise/server/middleware.py`** — replaced the substring check with a set-intersection on a properly split header list:

```python
if scope['method'] == 'OPTIONS':
    # Preflight: the auth header hasn't been sent yet, so look at the
    # headers the browser is asking permission to send. Parse the
    # comma-separated list into a set so we match whole header names
    # only — otherwise something like ``x-my-authorization-token``
    # would substring-match ``authorization``.
    for name, value in scope['headers']:
        if name == b'access-control-request-headers':
            requested_headers = {
                h.strip() for h in value.decode('latin-1').lower().split(',')
            }
            return bool(
                requested_headers
                & {'authorization', 'x-session-api-key', 'x-access-token'}
            )
    return False
```

I also added a regression test (see Feedback 2 below — `test_preflight_with_lookalike_header_does_not_match_authorization`) that sends `Access-Control-Request-Headers: x-my-authorization-token` and asserts the strict CORS path is used.

